HOW ENGLISH BECAME A DOUBLE LANGUAGE
After the Romans conquered England in ő century AD, they hired German and
Scandinavian mercenaries from Anglia and Saxony to help fend off pirates and put
down rebellions ł native Picts and Celts. When the Roman Empire abandoned
England in 410 AD, more Anglo-Saxons migrated ¤ island, marginalizing the
Gallic-speaking Celts, wiping out the Latin £ Romans, and imposing their
Germanic tongue throughout England.
But 600 years later Latin came back  this roundabout way: In 911 AD Danish
Vikings conquered territory along the north coast of France and named it after
themselves, Norma ndy, land £ Norsemen. After 150 years of marriage to French
women, these Danes spoke whµir mothers spoke, a thousand-year-old French
dialect of Latin. In 1066 King Wilhelm of Normandy (a.k.a. William the Conqueror) led
his armie s across the English Channel and defeated the English king. With that
victory , French came to England.
Throughout hiı foreign conquests usually erase native languages. But
England ả exception. For some mysterious reason, the Germanic language of
the Anglo-Saxons ¨ Latinate French £ Normans merged. Í the
vocabulary of what became modern English doubled. English has at least two words
for everything. Compare, ¹ the Germanic-rooted words “fire,” “hand,” “tip,”
“ham,” and “flow” ¤ French-derived words “flame,” “palm,” “point,” “pork,” and
“fluid.”
Given th e massive vocabulary of English, I of fer this guiding principle:
