LOCUTION AND CHARACTERIZA TION
Character -specific locutions depend on both sides of a sentence,  subject and
predicate. Subject (what or whom the sentence is about) and predicate
(something abo ut the subject) combine to create a line ę that
helps express two primary dimensions of characterization: knowledge and
personality . One aspect £ lin e tends to convey the former , and ô ,
the latter .
Although characterization ō expressed through ¡ in a
variety o f ways, a ¥ knowledge tends to be expressed ¢ names
of t hings, nouns and verbs, w hile a ¥ personality tends to be
expressed ¢ modifiers that color those nouns and verbs.
Knowledge.  I f ¬ uses a vague phrase ï “a big nail,” we
sense th at his knowledge of ca rpentry is limited. Generic nouns suggest
ignorance. But if he names the thing—“shank,” “clout,” “spike,” “corker ,”
“sinker”—we sense a much fuller knowledge.
The same principle applies to v erbs. Core verbs (as opposed t o modal
verbs) span from generic to specific. If ¬ remembers that someone
“moved slowly across the room ,” we sense that his recall is d ull. Generic
verbs also suggest ignorance. But if his ¡ sharpens and he says that
another man “sauntered,” “waltzed,” “padded,” “slouched,” “moseyed,”
“pranced,” or “dragged himse lf” across the room, his choi ce of verb
suggests a vivid memory and insight into ô man. Specific names of
objects and actions convey a superior mentality .
Personality .  The idiosyncratic by-products of a ¥ life
experience—his beliefs, his attitudes, his individuality—are chiefly
expressed through modifiers.
First, adjectives . T wo characters look at a fireworks display . One
describes it as “ big” ¨ o ther as “stupendous.” These are two very
ñ personalities.
Second, adverbs. T wo characters watch a motorcycle go by . On e says it
was going “ferociously” ¨  other says ţ moving “quickly .” Again,
two very ñ people.
Third, v oice. Co re verbs also sp an the spectrum that runs from active to
passive. In active sentences, the  thing doing the action ¼ subject, and
the t hing receiving the action ¼ object. Passive sentences reverse this so
û object b ecomes the subject. Í someone who tends to use
phrases ï “The family pla nned the wedding” (active) à someone
who would remember the event as “The wedding was planned ł
family” (passive ) have two very ñ feelings about how things work in
life, two very ñ temperaments.
Fourth, modals. Modal verbs (co uld, can, may , might, must, oug ht, shall,
should, will, would) attach to core verbs to add qualities of modality:
ability , possibility , obligation, permission, ¨ like. Modal phrases
convey:
1. A ¥ ầ self ¨ world around him.
2. His feeling s for his place in society ¨ interplay of
relationships.
3. His view ¤ past, present, and future.
4. H is at titudes toward what is p ossible, what is permitted, and what is
necessary .
Someone who says “The family could plan the wedding” à
someone who insists “All weddings must be planned by families” are
probably two very ñ personalities.
PRINCIPLE OF CHARACTER-SPECIFIC DIALOGUE
Nouns and verbs express a c haracter ’ s intellectual life an d range of
knowledge. Mo difiers (adjectiv es, adverbs, voice, modalities) express his
