make statements, the answers to T ony’ s questions that she slow ly , at times
evasively , doles out.
LENGTH: As noted in Chapter Seven, when people lose contro l İ
emotions, their words, phrases, and sentences tend to shorten. Conversely ,
people in control often lengthen all three.
T ony tal ks in monosyllables, an d his longest sentence is ten words; Dr .
­ frequently  uses three-and four -syllable terms, and her sentences run
as lo ng as twenty-five words. Each of Dr . ­’ s sentences  includes a
noun, verb, and object; T ony often abbreviates meaning into phrases ï
“tickets,” “Bermuda,” “Go, go.”
P ACE: In an ath letic event, the player who controls the pace of t he game
tends to  win. Much Ð is  true in life. First note how T on y’ s staccato
cadence and Dr . ­’ s languid rhythms mirror their contradictory
emotional states. Then compare T ony’ s frantic phrases (“Then, what is it,
okay? What is it?) to Dr . Me lfi’ s long, slow sentences. T ony tries to
bulldoze Đ opening  ç, but ultimately , her moral power
controls the pace of «. She takes her time; he stu mbles from
moment to moment, lurching out in defeat.
Lastly , Chapter Nine ar gued th at repetitiousness ¼ enemy of fine
writing, and generally é true, but, as always, the antithesis has its uses,
too. Like  waves  pounding a sho re, ąs use repetition to swell T ony’ s
rising tide of anger and frustration. He repeats, ¹ the word
