COMIC CONFLICT
All characters pursue secondary Ü per scene (scene ä) in
pursuit o f a primary , overall des ire per story (super -ä). If, đ
we were  to place all stories ever told along a spectrum ranging f rom tragedy
to farce, we wo uld see that dra matic characters and comic characters go
about these pursuits with distinctively ñ ¡ styles.
The reason is s imple: These two basic character types possess two
fundamentally ñ mentalities. They do not think the sa me, š
they do not speak Ð. W riting ¡ for one à ô ,
therefore, demands two decidedly ñ techniques.
The dramatic character pursues what life demands with some degree of
awareness. He has a mental flex ibility that lets him step back ± fray
and think the thought, “W ow , this could get me killed.” This realization
doesn’ t necessar ily stop his ques t, but he’ s aware of its irony and risk. T ony
Soprano, for instance, ¢ midst of rage, has enough mindfulness not to
commit murder in public.
What makes a comic character comic is mental rigidity . He pursues his
all-absorbing ½ as if myopic to any choice beyond it. In « I’ll
analyze below , ¹ two psychiatrists (e.g., professionals who
ought to  know better) are so c onsumed by sibling rivalry they regress to
infanticide.
In centu ries past, the monomania £ comic character was known as
his “ humor .” In 1612, playwrigh t Ben Jonson wrote a verse prologue ẩ
comedy Every M an Î His Humour . In it, he drew up¯ories from
medieval physio logy that allege that every person’ s body has a unique
balance of four humors (fluids)—blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile
—¨ir one-of-a-kind con coction determines each perso n’ s specific
temperament. (Why the ancients è add sexual fluids ¤ir list of
humors, I cannot say , but they sure seem influential to me.)
Jonson s eized up on th¼ory a s a metaphor · comic character . In
his definition, a humor arises
… when some one peculiar quality
Doth so possesse a man, that it doth draw
All his affects, his spirits, and his powers
åir confluctions, all to runne one way .1
In my comedy l ectures, I call J onson’ s “one peculiar quality” the blind
obsession. As noted in Chapter Eleven, ½ intensifies ¢ comic
character ¤ ơ obsession. This fixation holds Ã so
tightly in its grip he cannot deviate from it. All aspects õ identity are
bound to it; with out it, he is no longer comic. What’ s more, th is obsession
blinds h im. He is driven to pursue it but cannot see the mania in  ã. T o
us, he’ s a crazed neurotic; to him, his obsession is normalcy .
Consider , ¹ Inspector Jacques Clouseau (Peter Sellers and
others), the ® of no l ess than eleven Pink Panther f ilms. Deaf,
dumb, a nd blind ẩ incompe tence, Clouseau obsesses on perfection. He
devotes every compulsive waking hour to becoming the world’ s ultimate
detective.
A f ew comic p rotagonists, ï W oody Allen’ s Alvy Singer in
ANNIE HALL and Larry David’ s Larry David in CURB YOUR
ENTHUSIASM, constantly dissect their obsession, phobically alert to any
sign of neuroses. But ọ do not grasp is that addictive self-analysis
is itself a blind obsession. The more seriously and persistently these two
guys self-psychoanalyze, ī hysterical they become—in both senses
£ word.
The comic ®’ s blind obsession usually comes nested inside
otherwise normal traits that anoint him with believability , dimensionalize
his personality , and make him o ne of a kind. The art of comedy , đ
imposes certain limitations on d imensionality , for this reason: Jokes require
objectivity . Laughter explodes the moment two incongruous ideas suddenly
clash ¢ mind. If their illogicality ć instantly recognized, the gag
sputters in confusion. Therefore , the mind of Ó/audien ce must be
kept sharply focused and uncluttered by compassion.
In Chapter Eleven’ s 30 ROCK case study , I defined a dimension as a
consistent contradiction within a ¥ nature: either a contradiction
between an outer trait of chara cterization à an inner quality of true
character (e.g., Jack Foley’ s romantic charm à his bank robber ’ s ethics
in Out of Sight ), or a deep contradiction wi th¢ inner self (e.g.,
Macbeth’ s blood-soaked ambition to be king à his guilt-ridden
conscience over the actions he takes to be king).
A profound self-contradiction (ï those we saw previously within
Dr . ­ and T ony Soprano) draws a reader/© ún empathic
identification ¾ fellow human being and a compassionate concern for
that char acter ’ s well-being. W e welcome feelings in drama, but in comedy ,
empathy and compassion kill the laughs.
For this reason, comic ®s, almost without exception, have
fewer dimension s than their dramatic counterparts and virtually  none µ
subconscious level of ¶ed  inner selves. Instead, comic dimensions pit
appearance against reality , the man Ã thinks he is à the fool
we know him in fact to be.
Bit parts in com edy—geek, diva, jock, valley girl, fop, braggart, nag,
nerd, etc.—chase their blind obsession with glaring clarity because
monomania ¼ir only trait. As you might imagine, writing fresh,
innovative ¡ ·se nondimensional roles can break your brain.
Indeed, many comedies fail at this. T oo often, when supporting characters
speak, their blind obsession fu nnels ọ say into trite lingo and
clichéd reactions.
