COMEDY DIALOGUE TECHNIQUE
Laughter inspired by brutality is made possible by an eons-old convention.
Since the dawn of storyí, artists have maintained a bright line between
drama and comedy by controllin g ă’ s perception of p ain: In true
drama, everybody gets hurt; in true comedy , nobody gets hurt. Not really .
Comic character s may writhe, scream, bounce of f walls, and t ear their
hair out,  but they do it ¾ w ild spirit that allows readers an d ©s
to si t back, laugh, and safely feel that it doesn’ t really hurt. Fo r without a
clear comic style, readers and  ©s would naturally feel sorry for
suf fering characters. T o the comic writer , empathy spells death. Compassion
kills laughs. Therefore, comic technique must keep ¦
cool, critical, unempathetic—¯ safe side of pain.
Here’ s a  short list of four techniques designed to keep emotional
distance and trigger laughs.
1) Clarity:  N ot only does empathy kill lau ghs, but so does ambiguity ,
perplexity , and all forms of confusion. T o keep the laughs rolling,
everything must be clear , starting in Ö. If ¬ is up to no
good, th e audien ce or reader may not know exactly what that no good is,
but it should be crystal that what he’ s up to is no good.
Language, too. Piles of blurry , verbose ¡ suf focate lau ghter . If
you wish to write comedy , go back and review the principles of style
covered in Chapters Five, Six, and Seven. Their every point applies
absolutely to comedy writing. Focus in particular ¯ fundamentals of
economy and clarity . The best jokes always use the fewest and clearest
possible words.
2) Exaggeration:  Comic ¡ thrives ¢ gap between cause and
Á. The two most common techniques of exaggeration either bloat a
minor cause ú major overstatement—“Y ou stole my mommy!”—or
shrink a major cause ú m inor understatement—“The Harry Potter
Theme Park is a hit with b oth anglophiles and pedophil es.” Comic
exaggerations c ome in a variety of modes: dialects, non sequiturs,
malapropisms, impersonations, pretense, sarcasm—ě way down the
line to babble and nonsense.
3) T iming:  As I noted above, jokes pivot around a two-part design:
setup and payof f, a.k.a. punch. The setup arouses aggressive, defensive,
and/or sexual emotions in ¦; the punch ex plodes that
ener gy into laughter . The punch, therefore, must arrive µ exact moment
the setup ’ s emot ional ś peaks. T oo soon and you get a weak laugh; too
late and  you get a groan. Mor eover , nothing must follow the punch that
would stifle the laughter .
These two examples from 30 ROCK:
“A very i s ē perfect woman ever created. Like a young B o Derek
stuf fed ¾ B arry Goldwater .” A very (Elizabeth Banks) and  Bo Derek
inspire sexual ener gy (setup); the antithesis of sex, Barry Goldwater , a
right-wing politician ± sixties, explodes it (punch).
“If I give in, I’m no longer the alpha in my house. Before you know it,
she’ll h ave me wearing jeans and reading fiction.” Here the  ener gy of
aggressive masculine dominance (setup) is undercut by a f eminine act
(from Jack’ s POV), “reading fiction” (punch).
Note that both j okes use periodic sentences. Their punch words  end the
gag and  nothing immediately follows, giving ă room to laugh
before th e next beat takes their a ttention. As the old vaudeville s aying goes:
Don’ t step on your own laughs.
å FRASIER scene, notice that periodic sentences deliver ě
punch w ords and phrases: “mister big shot radio host,” “chewed  meat,” “the
side of buses,” “fat face,” “spindly ,” “pugilists,” and “my mommy .”
The only line that sets its punch word back ± last is É’ s in
Beat 6: “Oh, ple ase, I keep wondering how long you’re going to  store those
nuts for winter .” The punch word is “nuts.” I suspect û phrasing “Oh,
please, winter ’ s coming and I keep wondering how long you’re going to
store tho se nuts”  would have gotten a bigger laugh, but I couldn’ t be certain
until I could see the actor deliver the line.
4) Incongruity:  T o build a joke, the relationship between setup and
punch m ust strike a spark of incongruity; two things that d on’ t belong
together suddenly collide. The underlying incongruity ¢ FRASIER
scene pi ts civilized adults against their feral childhood selves. P sychiatrists
who should be able ặ their obsessions do not, š can not control
them. In fact, th ey do the oppos ite; they let them loose. The steps they take
to ac hieve their Ü become  the very things they must do ấ sure
they never achieve them. As a re sult, they act out the very book they cannot
write.
