Needless to say , í and showing call upon two very ñ ¡
techniques.
An ancie nt litera ry convention grants us the godlike power to overhear a
¥ ê, ě while knowing that she ć talking to us. If
so, then  to whom is she speaki ng? Dialogue, by definition, is a two-sided
exchange between a speaker and a listener . Who besides us is listening to
her? If ø talking to another s elf within herself, her mind mus t be dual. If
dual or even multiple, how does her mind divide itself into selves? Exactly
how many are there? Who are they? How do they link?
These q uestions are neither new nor unique to storyí. T we nty-five
hundred years ago, the Buddha taught his followers to ignore all such
wonderings because they flow ± false premise û self exists,
when in fact it does not. The se nse of “me,” he believed, is an illusionary
side Á of uncountable, ever -shifting physical and sensory forces.
About Ð time, Socrates ar gued the opposite view . He taught his
students û self ž exists, but țnot live a meaningful,
civilized life without making e very ef fort ť who you  are. å
centuries since, this debate over the nature of intrapersonal com munication
has swung back and forth betw een these two philosophical extremes and
remains unsettled at best.
Science, on ô hand, has taken a stand.1 L ike Socrates, modern
science strives ť the hidden nature of consciousness, but  at Ð
time, like Buddha, it feels misle d by intuition. While our sixth sense tells us
we have  a core of consciousness located behind our eyes, brain scan
researchers report that several ñ mental processes, mediated by
ñ brain regions, collabora te to create this illusion. No single, central,
physical, all-con trolling self exists; “me” is indeed a side Á. On the so-
called “hard question” of consciousness, neuroscience now favors Buddha
over Socrates but without a clear conclusion.2
Although science cannot ground consciousness ¢ physical, and
philosophy cannot locate it ¢ metaphysical, artists know exactly where
to fi nd the self. When art whistles, the self comes running ń bounding
dog. For ỉteller , the self makes its home in Ð subjective
reaches where it has always lived and thrived.
Delusional or not, consciousness of self ¼ essence of our humanity .
If, as science tells us, the self ć confined to a specific lobe £ brain
but emer ges Î a composite of sources, so what? It’ s my co mposite and
I l ike it. If, as philosophy claims, the unreliable self shifts and ch anges from
one day  ¤ n ext and, therefore, cannot be known absolutely , so what?
It’ s m y changing self and I enjo y watching it evolve, hopefully ú better
person.
For î writers striving to express the inner life, philosophy’ s
deductive logic and science’ s in ductive logic dull aesthetic insight. Neither
leverages the inside-out power £ subjective; neither creates the
meaningful emo tional experience £ self that every major work of story
