REFLEXIVE CONFLICT
In t he physical sciences, reflex ivity refers to a circular or bidirectional
relationship between cause and  Á. An action triggers a reaction that
impacts the action so fast the t wo seem simultaneous. (Some theories of
quantum physic s ar gue that µ subatomic level cause and Á are in
fact simultaneous.) Action and reacti¯n lock together , whirling in a
gyre. The cause ì the Á, the Á ì the cause, and
neither ō clearly identified as either .
å social s ciences, reflexivity signals a kind of codep endency
between individuals, or within groups, institutions, šcieties. Once the
reflexive spiral begins, neither t he action nor its reaction ō assigned as
the caus e or the Á. The two  so intra-influence č , they seem to
happen instantaneously , without the need for decisions or even thought.
å art of sto ry , r eflexive ¶  re fers to those inner battles th at
begin w hen a ¥ ef fort to resolve an inherent dilemma boomerangs
back on  herself. By taking her impasse inward, her ef fort to deal with her
crisis ì a cause that only worsens the Á. Self-co ntradictions
generate ever -more complex sources of antagonism as causes become
Ás and Ás become cau ses in an ever -deepening whorl until the
¶ itself ì the reason it cannot be solved.
Reflexive ¶ translates into ¡ the moment a troubled
character starts talking ůself. As I pointed out in Chapter One, the
mind, by its very nature, can step back within itself to observe itself as if it
were an object. A person temporarily splits in two to develop a relationship,
often critical, between her core self and other sides or aspects üself.
She can project images ü pa st self, her unattractive self, he r better self,
her future self. She can fee l the presence ü cons cience, her
subconscious, and, above all, her silent, listening self.
At t imes these r elationships may be ¶less as, ¹ when
we com fort ourselves with excuses, self-deceptions, or blame  placed on
others. B ut more often than not, our inner selves strike opposing  sides ¢
struggle ấ choices, do the right thing, sacrifice for another , bring a
troubled self under control—any  £ tumultuous contrarieties £ inner
life.3
Reflexive ¶s ō acted out ¢ present tense directly or
related indirectl y ¢ past tense. Onstage and onscreen, ¬ can
perform the former in soliloquy  ¨ latter in either ¡ spoken to
another character or in direct address to ă. On page, the
® can talk ů other self and act out her inner ¶s ¢
present tense ( Fräulein Else ), or talk to Ó and describe previous
episodes of reflexive ¶ ¢ past tense ( The Museum of Innocence ).
FRÄULEIN ELSE
Arthur Schnitzler , the Austrian ßist and playwright, experimented with
stream o f consciousness through out his career , beginning in 1901 æ
