immediately floo d her mind: to save her parents and sacrifice herself à
to save herself and sacrifice her parents. Either way she chooses, a great
price mu st be paid, because whe n I say “save herself,” I mean that literally .
Else’ s identity is tied ů mo rality . If she saves her family , she loses her
morality; if she loses her morality , she loses her identity .
T rapped by this double bind of irreconcilable goods/lesser of two evils,
Else’ s only path ű runs between her inner selves. So · rest of
