MINIMAL CONFLICT
INTRODUCTION: THE BALANCE OF TEXT AND
SUBTEXT
Every li ne ę strikes a balance between the literal meaning £
words said ¨ unsaid m eanings that ¦ senses
resounding through Ö.
When this balance tilts ș a minor motivation causes a massive
vocal response, ¡ sounds  hollow and « feels for ged. Recall
the defin ition of melodrama from Chapter Six: the overwrought expression
of limp needs. Like chefs who use rich sauces to hide spoiled food, writers
who wh ip up blubbery stylistics  to smother stale content risk the sour odor
of melodrama.
When b alance levels out ș  unsaid ð feelings transcribe
directly and fully into what is said, we call this blatancy wri ting on-the-
nose. If a Â implicit and explicit meanings echo each ot her , ²
turns into text, depth dries up, lines sound tinny , ¨ acting clanks.
When the balance favors content over form, when minimal words
express maximal meaning, dialo gue gains its greatest credibility and power .
Using R obert B rowning’ s famous phrase, “Less is more.”1 A ny word that
ō cut should be cut, especially if its loss adds ¤ line’ s Á.
Sparseness of language gives th e reader or © a chance t o peer ever
more deeply º unsaid a nd unsayable. W ith few exceptions, when
understatement takes the upper hand, ¡ resonates.
What fo llows is  a superb examp le £ fewest possible words  used to
express the maximum possible feeling and meaning.
LOST IN TRANSLA TION
Sofia Coppola wrote and directe d LOST IN TRANSLA TION when she was
thirty-two. It was her fourth script to reach the screen and her second
feature to direct. The film w on numerous awards around the world,
including an Oscar for Best Original Screenplay .
Coppola grew up surrounded by  artists, she has traveled to Japan many
times, and, ńll of us, she has won and lost at love. So although it’ s fair
to assume that personal experience influenced Coppola’ s storyí, it
would be a mis take to treat LOST IN TRANSLA TION as aut obiography .
Rather , h er writi ng expresses the widely read, finely tuned mind  of an artist
who uses fiction to express her insights into life’ s secret places.
In Coppola’ s minimalist writing, ¡ progression does not use
ar guments to sling words like st ones (the conjugal hostilities of A Raisin in
the Sun ) or manipulations to weave words ú trap (À malicious
feigning in The Gr eat Gatsby ). Instead, subtle, indirect, sparse language
shapes implicit,  virtually invisible but deeply felt ¶s that haunt her
characters out İ pasts and º present.
In th e sc ene bel ow , Coppola works º inner selves and pa st selves
of h er characters simultaneously . She reveals both in each without resorting
to choices under the pressure of dilemma.
Choices between  irreconcilable goods ¨ lesser of two ev ils, once
poised, are profoundly revealin g of true character but relatively easy to
dramatize. Coppola takes another path: Her characters are not in ¶
with wh at life imposes but æ emptiness of what life d enies. This
quiet scene hooks, holds, and p ays of f without direct, indirect, or reflexive
¶.
SETUP
The key  to creating ể of minimum ¡ and maximu m impact is
its s etup. If, prior to ể, ỉí has brought charac ters to crisis
